      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "The primary prevention of coronary artery disease in patients with diabetes could have a large impact on health care costs and outcomes. Guidelines for improving diabetic health indices are common, but significant challenges exist in implementing them."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "GENERAL QUESTION",
          "content": "How does integrating an evidence-based guideline into an electronic medical record affect patient care?"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "SPECIFIC RESEARCH CHALLENGE",
          "content": "How can we implement the new guideline-enhanced medical record in a controlled manner and measure its impact on physician satisfaction, diabetes process measures, and the risk for cardiovascular disease?"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "PROPOSED APPROACH",
          "content": "All patients in the University of Washington system have an electronic Web-based medical record. Patients with diabetes will be randomly assigned to a guideline-enhanced or standard electronic medical record. The electronic medical record allows measurement of most clinical process measures and outcomes. Physician satisfaction will be measured by survey."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "POTENTIAL DIFFICULTIES",
          "content": "Contamination may occur when guideline recommendations are applied to control patients as physicians gain experience with the guideline-enhanced record."
        }
      ]},
